-- title:
--   Sign invariance of `IsLevelAutAt` in γ
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero, $n, m \in \mathbb{N}$ with $m \neq 0$, $\zeta \in L$, $N_0 \in \mathbb{N}$, $H$ a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/N_0)^\times$, $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, $K$ an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$ (the Laurent series over $L$) and $\tau$ an $L$-algebra automorphism of $K$. The predicate [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L n ζ m N₀ H γ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) asserts: for every weight $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, every pair of modular forms $f, g$ of weight $k$ for the subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$ determined by $\Gamma_H(N_0) \leq \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ (the preimage of $H$ under the reduction map $\Gamma_0(N_0) \to (\mathbb{Z}/N_0)^\times$), every pair of integral power series $p_f, p_g \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ whose images in $\mathbb{C}[[q]]$ are the $q$-expansions of $f$ and $g$ at width $1$, with the image of $p_g$ in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ nonzero, every $x \in K$ whose image in $L((q))$ is the coefficientwise image of the ratio of the images of $p_f$ and $p_g$ in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, and every ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = \exp(2\pi i/n)$, one has
--   $$\iota_*\big(\tau(x)\big)\cdot \mathrm{qExp}_1\big(g \mid_k \gamma^{\sharp}\big) = \mathrm{qExp}_1\big(f \mid_k \gamma^{\sharp}\big)$$
--   in $\mathbb{C}((q))$, where $\iota_*$ is coefficientwise application of $\iota$ and $\gamma^{\sharp} = \mathrm{conjElemN}\,m\,\gamma$ is the element $\mathrm{diag}(m,1)^{-1}\gamma\,\mathrm{diag}(m,1)$ of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$, i.e. $\begin{pmatrix} a & b/m \\ mc & d\end{pmatrix}$ for $\gamma = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d\end{pmatrix}$. The theorem states that this predicate holds for $-\gamma$ if and only if it holds for $\gamma$.
--
--   The statement records that the condition defining a level automorphism attached to $\gamma$ depends only on the image of $\gamma$ in $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, the slash action of $-I$ in weight $k$ being multiplication by $(-1)^k$, which cancels in the ratios occurring in the definition. It is used downstream in the level-one auxiliary analysis of the modular curve — the blow-up chart and Drinfeld-fibre computations and the counting of the group of level automorphisms generated by a given $\gamma$ — where $-I$ lies in the relevant congruence subgroup at residue characteristic $2$.
